==Match==
===Summary===
The start of the match was dominated by Juventus, while some of the young Ajax players committed various errors in this phase.<ref name="UEFA">{{cite web|url=http://it.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=1995/matches/round=69/match=52534/postmatch/report/index.html|title=La Juve piega l'Ajax dal dischetto|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160407071134/http://it.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=1995/matches/round=69/match=52534/postmatch/report/index.html|archivedate=7 April 2016}}</ref> In the 12th minute, Ajax defender [[Frank de Boer]] clumsily headed the ball while falling back, allowing Juventus striker [[Fabrizio Ravanelli]] to quickly get between de Boer and goalkeeper [[Edwin van der Sar]], who was coming out of his net, and despite the very angled position, slid in the net to give Juventus the lead.<ref name="UEFA"/>

Ajax responded well, pushing further towards Juventus' half, taking advantage of their physical strength.<ref name=CIHEFE>{{cite web|language=es|author=José del Olmo|url=https://www.cihefe.es/cuadernosdefutbol/2018/04/xli-liga-de-campeones-199596-juventus-fc-torino-italia/|title=XLI Liga de Campeones 1995/96: Juventus FC Torino (Italia)|publisher=Cuadernos de Fútbol|number=97|editor=Centro de Investigaciones de Historia y Estadística del Fútbol Español|date=1 April 2018|ISSN=1989-6379}}</ref> From a corner kick, [[Nwankwo Kanu]] forced the Juventus goalkeeper [[Angelo Peruzzi]] to make a tough save,<ref name="UEFA"/> only for Ajax to later get a free kick in the 41st minute that surprised Peruzzi<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.ultimouomo.com/classici-ajax-juventus-96/|title=Classici: Ajax-Juventus '96|date=23 May 1996|language=Italian}}</ref> who only managed to block the ball which fell at the feet of [[Jari Litmanen]] to tap in to equalise.<ref name="UEFA"/> Just before half-time, [[Antonio Conte]] was subbed out due to injury, replaced with [[Vladimir Jugović]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/3566876/2022/09/07/antonio-conte-champions-league/|title=Antonio Conte’s painful relationship with the Champions League|publisher=nytimes.com|date=7 September 2022}}</ref>

The two teams went into half-time at 1–1, a score that remained unchanged in the second half where the only clear chance was for Juventus captain [[Gianluca Vialli]], who from a very good position shot just wide of the net,<ref name="UEFA"/> as well as a miss from [[Angelo Di Livio]].<ref name=CIHEFE/>

After extra-time, the match remained 1–1 and went to penalty shoot-out.<ref name="UEFA"/> The only clear chance in the extra-time was an opportunity missed by Juventus player [[Alessandro Del Piero]] in the second half.<ref name=CIHEFE/> In the penalty shoot-out, all four Juventus penalty takers scored, with those being [[Ciro Ferrara]], [[Gianluca Pessotto]], [[Michele Padovano]] and Jugović. For Ajax, [[Edgar Davids]] the first penalty taker's shot was saved by Peruzzi. The next two players scored, those being Litmanen and [[Arnold Scholten]]. Their final penalty taken by [[Sonny Silooy]] was also saved. Jugović's goal was the decisive penalty that gave Juventus its second title in the competition; Juventus won by a score of 4–2.<ref name="UEFA"/>

